.TH XCOMM 1 "2026-11-18" "xcomm 0.1.3" "User Commands"
.ad l
.nh
.SH NAME
xcomm \- a fast serial terminal with a control socket for scripts and agents
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xcomm
.RI [ options ]
.I device
.br
.B xcomm \-\-headless
.RI [ options ]
.I device
.br
.B xcomm ctl
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I device
\fBstatus\fR|\fBsend\fR|\fBread\fR|\fBwait\fR|\fBrun\fR|\fBbreak\fR|\fBdtr\fR|\fBrts\fR|\fBset\fR|\fBcapture\fR|\fBxfer\fR
.RI ...
.br
.B xcomm ctl
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.B sessions
.br
.B xcomm ctl \-\-pane
.I id
.RI ...
.br
.B xcomm list
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B xcomm pick
.br
.B xcomm watch
.RB [ start | stop | status | run ]
.br
.B xcomm \-\-skill
.br
.B xcomm skill install
.RB [ \-\-dir
.IR dir ]
.br
.B xcomm uninstall
.br
.B xcomm doctor
.br
.B xcomm man
.br
.B xcomm \-\-show\-prefix
[\fB\-\-prefix\fR \fIkey\fR | \fB\-m\fR]
.br
.B xcomm
.BR \-h " | " \-\-help " | " \-V " | " \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xcomm
connects the terminal to a serial device: what is typed goes to the device,
what the device sends is shown. Its options and keys follow
.BR minicom (1),
with a command prefix that stays out of the way of terminal multiplexers.
.PP
A running session keeps the last part of the device's output in memory and
listens on a control socket, so that another program \(em a script, a test
harness or a coding agent \(em can send to the device, wait for output, read
it, change line settings or transfer files, while the user watches the same
console.
.PP
.I device
is a name in
.I /dev
(for example
.BR ttyUSB0 )
or, with a
.B /
in it, a path (for example
.IR /dev/serial/by\-id/... ).
.B \-D
.I device
gives it as an option. Put
.B \-\-
before a name that starts with
.B \-
or is one of the command words
.BR ctl ", " list ", " pick ", " watch ", " skill ", " uninstall ", " doctor " or " man .
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Line settings
.TP
.BI \-b " baud"
Baud rate, default 115200. Any rate the driver accepts, not only the standard
ones.
.TP
.BR \-\-databits " 5|6|7|8"
Data bits, default 8.
.TP
.BR \-\-parity " none|even|odd"
Parity, default none.
.TP
.BR \-\-stopbits " 1|2"
Stop bits, default 1.
.TP
.BR \-\-flow " none|rts|xon"
Flow control: RTS/CTS or XON/XOFF, default none.
.SS Port
.TP
.B \-o
Leave DTR and RTS alone: xcomm does not raise them (the kernel may, as the
port opens) or drop DTR on exit.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-lock
Take no UUCP lock file. The port is still opened exclusively.
.TP
.B \-\-low\-latency
FTDI adapters on Linux: set the 1 ms latency timer (needs write access to its
sysfs file).
.SS Display
.TP
.BR \-\-color " auto|always|never"
Colours in device output, and the dim style of xcomm's
.B [ctl]
line. With
.B auto
(the default) they are kept on a terminal and removed when the output is
piped or
.BR TERM=dumb ;
.B NO_COLOR
also takes the dim style off, but not the device's colours.
.TP
.BR \-c " on|off"
The same as
.BR "\-\-color always|never" .
.TP
.B \-w
Turn line wrap on.
.TP
.BI \-C " file"
Append device output to
.IR file .
.TP
.BI \-\-ring\-size " bytes"
Device output kept in memory for the control socket: 4k to 1g, default 1m
(k, m and g are KiB, MiB and GiB).
.SS Control socket
.TP
.BR \-\-ctl " rw|ro|off"
What the session's socket allows: everything (the default), status and
reading only, or no socket at all.
.TP
.B \-\-headless
No terminal: device output goes only to the socket's clients and to
.BR \-C .
The session runs until SIGTERM, SIGINT or SIGHUP. Not with
.BR "\-\-ctl off" .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-ctl\-marker
Do not show what clients send as a
.B [ctl]
line in the terminal (dim while colour is on, see
.BR \-\-color " and " NO_COLOR ).
.SS Keys
.TP
.BI \-\-prefix " key"
The command prefix:
.BI ctrl\- letter
(not h, i, j or m) or
.BI alt\- letter\fR.
Also
.BR \-e ", " \-\-escape .
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-metakey
The same as
.BR "\-\-prefix alt\-a" .
.TP
.BI \-\-prefix\-time " ms"
How long a key cut short (Esc, Alt keys, key sequences) waits for the rest of
it: 1 to 1000, default 25.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-prefix
Print the prefix a session would use here, and why.
.SS Other
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the help, with the prefix in effect here.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH PREFIX KEY
The prefix is the first of:
.BR \-\-prefix ;
.B XCOMM_PREFIX
(which must be valid even when
.B \-\-prefix
is given); Alt\-A inside herdr, the Herdr app, tmux, screen or zellij;
otherwise Ctrl\-A, as in
.BR minicom .
The banner says which key is in effect and why.
.PP
Alt is recognised as Esc followed by the letter in the same read, and in the
CSI\-u form sent by terminals using the kitty keyboard protocol or xterm's
modifyOtherKeys. A lone Esc goes to the device after
.BR \-\-prefix\-time .
.PP
Multiplexer variables do not cross
.BR ssh (1)
and are removed by
.BR sudo (8),
so inside a multiplexer on another host the automatic rule picks Ctrl\-A.
Set
.B XCOMM_PREFIX=alt\-a
in the remote profile.
.SH COMMANDS
Typed after the prefix, with a terminal on standard input (piped input goes
to the device as it is):
.TP
.B z
Help.
.TP
.BR x ", " q
Quit.
.TP
.B f
Send a break.
.TP
.B h
Hang up: drop DTR for 500 ms.
.TP
.BR d ", " t
Toggle DTR, toggle RTS.
.TP
.B p
Change the baud rate.
.TP
.B e
Toggle local echo.
.TP
.B w
Toggle line wrap.
.TP
.B l
Start or stop capturing to a file.
.TP
.BR s ", " r
Send or receive files with zmodem, ymodem or xmodem (through lrzsz).
.TP
The prefix twice
Sends the prefix key itself.
.TP
.B Esc
Cancel.
.SH CONTROL SOCKET
Each session listens on
.IB $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR /xcomm/ tty .sock
(or
.IB /tmp/xcomm\- uid / tty .sock
when there is no usable runtime directory), reachable only by the same user.
.B xcomm ctl
talks to it:
.TP
.BI "xcomm ctl " device " status " \fR[\fIfield\fR]
Port, line settings, modem lines and output offsets. With a
.IR field ,
the name of a key of
.BR "\-\-json status" ,
only its value:
.B offset
is the number to give a later
.B wait
or
.B read
as
.BR \-\-since ,
.B pid
the session's process.
.TP
.BI "xcomm ctl " device " send " text
Send
.I text
and Enter (CR);
.B \-n
for no line ending,
.B \-\-lf
or
.B \-\-crlf
for another;
.BI \-\-hex " hex"
or
.BI \-\-file " path"
send bytes as they are. A client that closes the socket gives up the request it
was waiting on; requests still unread then (a pipe into
.BR socat ,
say) run anyway. Such a client is dropped, with what it has not run yet, only
once the device has taken nothing at all for 30 seconds.
.TP
.BI "xcomm ctl " device " read"
The device output the session keeps (escape sequences removed when piped, see
.BR \-\-color ),
from
.BI \-\-since " offset"
or the last
.BI \-\-last " bytes" \fR,
at most
.BI \-\-max\-bytes " n" \fR;
.B \-\-from\-oldest
starts at the oldest byte kept. The next offset goes to standard error as
.BI next= offset .
Where standard error goes to the same place as standard output (both
are terminals,
.B 2>&1
into a pipe or file, or the same file opened for appending), a line break goes to standard error first when the
output does not end its line, so that line starts on its own. Standard
output is never changed. A pipe into a program that shows it on the
terminal
.RB ( "| tee" )
is not known to go there, and gets no line break.
.TP
.BI "xcomm ctl " device " wait " regex
Wait for device output to match
.IR regex ,
from
.BI \-\-since " offset"
or from now on, and print the match; the offset after it goes to standard
error as
.BI end= offset .
Escape sequences are removed first unless
.BR \-\-raw .
A match spans at most 64 KiB.
.BI \-\-timeout " s"
(default 10, 0 for none) exits 1;
.BI \-\-fail\-on " regex"
(repeatable) exits 7 when it matches first;
.BI \-\-idle " ms"
ends the wait once the device has been that quiet.
.BR \ew ", " \ed ", " \es " and " (?i)
are ASCII;
.B \-\-unicode
makes them Unicode\-aware in a build with
.BR "\-\-features unicode" .
.TP
.BI "xcomm ctl " device " run " text " \-\-until " regex
Send
.I text
as
.B send
does, then wait as
.B wait
does from the offset of the send (or
.B \-\-idle
instead of
.BR \-\-until ),
and print the output between the echoed line and the match. More than
.BI \-\-max\-bytes " n"
(default 16 MiB) of it exits 2.
Its
.B end=
line is kept apart from the output as
.BR read 's
is.
.TP
.BI "xcomm ctl " device " break " \fR[\fIms\fR]
Send a break: default 250 ms, at most 5000.
.TP
\fBxcomm ctl\fR \fIdevice\fR \fBdtr\fR|\fBrts on\fR|\fBoff\fR|\fBtoggle\fR|\fBpulse\fR [\fIms\fR]
Set a modem line;
.B pulse
holds the other state for
.I ms
(default 500), then puts it back.
.TP
.BI "xcomm ctl " device " set " name = value ...
Change line settings:
.BR baud ", " databits ", " parity ", " stopbits ", " flow .
.TP
.BI "xcomm ctl " device " capture start " file " \fR|\fB capture stop"
Start or stop capturing on the session.
.TP
\fBxcomm ctl\fR \fIdevice\fR \fBxfer send\fR|\fBrecv zmodem\fR|\fBymodem\fR|\fBxmodem\fR [\fIfile\fR...]
Run lrzsz on the session's port. The reply comes when it ends; Ctrl\-C
aborts it.
.TP
.B xcomm ctl sessions
The running sessions.
.TP
.BI "xcomm ctl \-\-pane " id " ..."
The session in herdr pane
.IR id ,
instead of naming the device.
.TP
.B \-\-json
The reply as one JSON object (see
.BR EXAMPLES ).
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
For
.BR read ", " wait " and " run :
no
.B next=
or
.B end=
line on standard error, for output piped on (into
.BR tee ,
say) that stderr would otherwise run into on the terminal.
.SH OTHER COMMANDS
.TP
.B xcomm list
The serial ports: tty, USB VID:PID, product, the lock holder, the herdr pane
of an xcomm session on it, and the by\-id name.
.TP
.B xcomm pick
That list for herdr's popup: Enter opens the port in a new pane (or goes to
the pane that has it),
.B b
sets the baud,
.B o
the placement.
.TP
.BR "xcomm watch " [ start | stop | status | run ]
Tell herdr when a serial port comes or goes.
.B start
runs it in the background, as the herdr plugin does
.RB ( \-\-daemon
is the same). It reads
.B notify
and
.B ignore
from
.IR $HERDR_PLUGIN_CONFIG_DIR/config.toml ;
its pid file and log are under
.BR $HERDR_PLUGIN_STATE_DIR ,
in a directory of each herdr server's own (named after its socket), so each
server runs a watcher.
.TP
.B xcomm \-\-skill
Print the agent skill: how an agent drives a session with
.BR "xcomm ctl" .
.TP
.BI "xcomm skill install " \fR[\fB\-\-dir\fI " dir" \fR]
Write the skill as
.I SKILL.md
in
.IR dir ,
by default
.IR ~/.agents/skills/xcomm ,
replacing only a copy xcomm wrote. The default is refused where a dotfiles
repository may own it.
.TP
.B xcomm uninstall
Take away what an install made, in order, saying how each step went: the
hotplug watchers, the skill, the herdr plugin, then the binaries
.RB ( "cargo uninstall" ),
with the man page in
.I ~/.local/share/man/man1
if xcomm wrote it.
It stops at the first failure and says what is left.
.TP
.B xcomm doctor
Report what is installed and what is left over (stale sockets and locks, a
skill, man page or watcher with no binary); exit 1 when something needs attention.
.TP
.B xcomm man
Show this page, which is built into xcomm, in
.BR man (1).
Piped, write its source instead:
.BR "xcomm man > xcomm.1" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
A session exits 0 after quit, the end of input, SIGTERM, SIGINT or SIGHUP,
or when the terminal goes away; 1 on an error or when the device goes away;
2 on a usage error or when the port is in use.
.PP
.B xcomm ctl
exits:
.TP
.B 0
ok
.TP
.B 1
.BR wait / run
timed out, or another error
.TP
.B 2
usage error
.TP
.B 3
no session for the device
.TP
.B 4
port disconnected
.TP
.B 5
busy (a file transfer)
.TP
.B 6
offset evicted from the ring
.TP
.B 7
a
.B \-\-fail\-on
pattern matched
.TP
.B 8
permission denied
.RB ( "\-\-ctl ro" ,
or a socket that is not the user's)
.TP
.B 9
subscriber dropped (fell behind)
.PP
Every other command exits 0 on success, 1 on an error and 2 on a usage
error.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XCOMM_PREFIX
The command prefix, as for
.BR \-\-prefix .
.TP
.B XCOMM_NO_LOCK
.B 1
is the same as
.BR \-\-no\-lock ,
for where options cannot be given (the herdr plugin's session pane, on a
system where no lock directory is the user's to write: set it before herdr
starts);
.B 0
or unset takes the lock.
.TP
.B TERM
.B dumb
removes colours with
.BR "\-\-color auto" .
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Set and not empty, with
.BR "\-\-color auto" :
the
.B [ctl]
line is not dim. The device's output keeps its colours: it is the board's,
not xcomm's.
.TP
.BR HERDR_ENV ", " HC_TERMINAL_ID ", " TMUX ", " STY ", " ZELLIJ
A multiplexer: the automatic prefix is Alt\-A.
.TP
.BR HERDR_SOCKET_PATH ", " HERDR_PANE_ID
Inside herdr, the session gives its pane a title and metadata (port and
baud).
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Where the control sockets go, when it is a directory of the user's.
.TP
.BR HERDR_PLUGIN_STATE_DIR ", " HERDR_PLUGIN_CONFIG_DIR
Set by herdr for
.BR "xcomm watch" .
.TP
.BR HOME ", " XDG_STATE_HOME ", " CARGO_HOME ", " CARGO_INSTALL_ROOT
Where
.BR "skill install" ", " uninstall " and " doctor
look.
.TP
.BR GIT_DIR ", " GIT_WORK_TREE
When set,
.B skill install
refuses its default directory: it may be a dotfiles repository's.
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_DATA_HOME
Where
.B skill install
looks for the yadm and vcsh repositories that may manage
.BR $HOME .
.SH FILES
.TP
\fB$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/xcomm/\fItty\fB.sock\fR, \fB/tmp/xcomm\-\fIuid\fB/\fItty\fB.sock\fR
Control sockets.
.TP
\fI/run/lock/lockdev\fR, \fI/var/lock/lockdev\fR, \fI/run/lock\fR, \fI/var/lock\fR, \fI/var/spool/lock\fR (Linux); \fI/var/spool/lock\fR, \fI/var/lock\fR, \fI/tmp\fR (macOS)
UUCP lock files,
.BI LCK.. tty\fR,
in the first of these that can be written. On Linux they are shared with
.BR minicom ", " picocom
and the like; stock macOS has no shared lock directory, so there they keep
xcomm sessions apart and the exclusive open does the rest.
.TP
.I ~/.agents/skills/xcomm/SKILL.md
The agent skill, as
.B xcomm skill install
writes it.
.SH EXAMPLES
Wait for a line that comes after a reboot, from the offset taken before it:
.PP
.EX
off=$(xcomm ctl ttyUSB0 status offset)
xcomm ctl ttyUSB0 send reboot
xcomm ctl ttyUSB0 wait 'login:' \-\-since "$off" \-\-timeout 120
.EE
.PP
Whole replies as JSON, for a parser such as
.BR jq (1):
.PP
.EX
xcomm ctl \-\-json ttyUSB0 status | jq .settings
xcomm ctl \-\-json ttyUSB0 run 'uname \-r' \-\-until '\er?\en[^\en]*# $' \-\-timeout 5 | jq \-r .output
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR minicom (1),
.BR picocom (1),
.BR tio (1),
.BR sz (1),
.BR rz (1)
